    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1156">

                <pb facs="0956" n="946" xml:id="pg946"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">119.2/3–1449: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hong Kong</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hopper</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hong Kong</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-03-14T14:00:00+08:00">March 14, 1949—2
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 15—3:11 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>91. In cover received today Chiao Mu returned Dept’s message left with him March
                    10, with personal informal signed note dated March 12 to Vice Consul
                        Service<anchor n="28a" xml:id="d1156-f1"/> explaining “very sorry that I
                    cannot forward such a message”. Tone of note was friendly.</p>
                <p>Return of message coincides with Peiping action and Consulate assumes text
                    message radioed Communist headquarters.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 91, repeated Nanking 21, AmEmb Canton 7, Shanghai 26, Peiping
                    unnumbered.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hopper</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1156-f1" n="28a" xml:id="d1156-f1-content">
                <p>Richard M. Service.</p>
            </note>
